{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And you, take for yourself.</b> Like לך לך (Genesis 12:1), and וקחו לכם צאן (above, 12:21). And according to the plain sense, similarly עשה לך שרף (Numbers 21:8), also פסל לך (below, 34:1), for such is the way of the language:" "And the word מר is difficult, and the Gaon [Saadia] translated it מוש\"ק [musk]. But it is not a spice [מעצים], even though it has a good fragrance — perhaps because of this Scripture separated it from the spices, for it is written אריתי מורי עם בשמי (Song of Songs 5:1), and it is gathered. And those who bring it say that it is produced in the throat of the gazelle. And it is written וידי נטפו מור (Song of Songs 5:5) — perhaps so it is when it is moist. n.: מעצים is read here as 'spice' (from עצם), though the word is unusual; the translation reflects this reading." "<b>And the sense of בשמים ראש.</b> That the head [finest part] of every spice be taken, for it is the most distinguished of what remains at the end. And after it says קנמן בשם [cinnamon, a spice], what reason to mention it? Perhaps because the cane [קנה] too should be from the choicest. And our predecessors of blessed memory said that the weight of all is equal and so we accept it, only we do not know a reason why it mentions half of it, and the reason for the halves I do not understand, for there is no custom in all the land of Ishmael to add to anything that is weighed even the weight of a mustard seed, much less in all the work of a perfumer."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:39:29.268069284Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}